{"genre":"Essay","passages":[{"id":"T1","text":"The pandemic has certainly thrown a wrench into our daily routines, causing many to become more sedentary. Working from home, attending virtual meetings, and spending hours in front of screens have become the new norm for many. Meanwhile, the closure of gyms, sports facilities, and recreational centers has further restricted access to structured exercise. As a result, people are finding themselves moving less, which can lead to issues like weight gain, an increased risk of chronic diseases, and weaker immune systems.

Psychologically, the pandemic has also had its toll. Uncertainty, anxiety, and stress have prompted some to turn to more sedentary activities like binge-watching TV shows or playing video games as ways to cope. However, maintaining physical activity is crucial for mental health. Exercise releases endorphins, those feel-good chemicals produced by our brains, which can help combat symptoms of depression and anxiety. Engaging in regular physical activity thus plays a vital role in both physical and mental well-being."},{"id":"T2","text":"The pandemic has certainly messed up our daily routines, pushing many of us into a more sedentary lifestyle. With folks working from home, joining virtual meetings, and staring at screens for longer periods, there's been a noticeable drop in physical activity. To make matters worse, the closure of gyms, sports facilities, and other recreational spots has made it even harder to get moving. This lack of regular exercise can lead to some serious health issues, including weight gain, a higher risk of chronic diseases, and a weakened immune system.

Moreover, the mental strain brought on by the pandemic—uncertainty, anxiety, and stress—has driven many to seek solace in less active pursuits like binge-watching TV shows or gaming. Yet, staying physically active is crucial for mental well-being. Exercise helps reduce stress, boosts mood, and can even alleviate symptoms of depression and anxiety by triggering the release of endorphins, those natural feel-good chemicals in our brains."},{"id":"T3","text":"First, the pandemic has disrupted daily life, leading to sedentary habits. Many people work from home, attend virtual meetings, and spend too much time in front of screens. In addition, the closing of gyms, sports and leisure centers has limited access to organized exercise. As a result, people are moving less, which can have a negative effect on health, such as weight gain, increased risk of chronic diseases and weakened immunity. Secondly, the psychological impact of the pandemic has led to a decrease in physical activity. The uncertainty, the anxiety, the fear that the pandemic has caused, have led people to seek comfort in sedentary activities, such as watching series or playing games. But exercise is essential for mental health, to reduce stress and to improve mood. Exercise triggers the release of endorphins, the brain's natural happy chemicals, which can relieve depression and anxiety."},{"id":"T4","text":"First, the pandemic has disrupted daily routines, leading to sedentary habits. Many people work from home, attend virtual meetings, and spend too much time in front of screens. Moreover, the closing of gyms, sports and leisure centers has reduced access to organized physical exercise. As a result, people are moving less, which can have adverse health consequences such as weight gain, increased risk of chronic diseases and weakened immunity. Secondly, the psychological impact of the pandemic has also led to a decrease in physical activity. The uncertainty, anxiety and stress associated with the pandemic have led people to cope with sedentary activities such as binge-watching series or playing video games. However, physical activity is essential for mental health, to reduce stress and improve mood. It releases endorphins, the brain’s natural feel-good chemicals, which can help to relieve symptoms of depression and anxiety."}]}

